  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 AM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nThe forecast continues to be very straight-forward through the \nnext week, and confidence is therefore high in the forecast \ndetails. Global model ensembles agree that an anomalously warm \nupper level ridge will slowly shift eastward across the lower \nMississippi Valley region as we go through this weekend and into \nnext week. This will lead to a continuation of dry weather with \nlarge diurnal temperature ranges of near 40 deg F.\n\nOf greatest importance, the continued drying of vegetation\n(Extreme KBDI values > 700) combined with exceptionally low \ndaytime RH values (< 25%) will help to maintain an elevated fire \ndanger threat. We will continue to message this through the \ngraphics and fire danger statement. Given the anticyclonic\nlow level flow, light wind will help to limit the overall \nfire danger threat levels. \n\nOtherwise, rainfall is not expected until the end of next week at\nthe earliest, when the ridge shows signs of breaking down, and \nwhen the subtropical stream influence increases over our region. \nEven then, global ensembles show the greater rainfall signal \ncentered more to our west, so confidence is not great for drought \nrelief anytime soon.\n\nLooking well ahead, all interests should monitor for increased \nfire danger threats in strong southerly flow ahead of cold fronts.\n/EC/",
